To run the crawler:
scrapy crawl venom -o items.json

Venom will crawl all item's pages and collect their names and Ankama ID.
To run, uncomment the category you need, and update the amount of pages the Ankama website has for that category.

Venom-part2 needs to be run after Venom. It will use the file generated from venom and crawl each individual item, collecting its stats. Before running, you need to update lines 28 and 36 with the new file generated by Venom (if you know the IDs of the items you want added, you can skip venom, and just manually add the IDs in the list in line 115).

Set_bonus will crawl through all the sets and colect the items/bonus for each set. Check that the range ofIds in line 22 contains all sets that need updating.

Rosetta needs the whole site to be running. It collects the names of items in all other languages (the other scrapers do only English).

Class_items will collect class items, the ID's of the items need to be input by hand.

Never rebuild an item database while a test suite is running.

build_wakfu_db.py deletes items_wakfu.db before writing it, and the suite opens
that same file through structure.py. Rebuilding under a running suite gives it
a missing or half-written database, which shows up as failures that do not
reproduce.

Two suites CAN run at the same time. This was measured rather than assumed, and
the belief that they could not was wrong: Django gives an in-memory test
database the name file:memorydb_default?mode=memory&cache=shared, and
cache=shared shares between connections OF ONE PROCESS, never between
processes. A parent that creates a table on that URI and holds the connection
open sees it; a child process opening the same URI sees nothing. So two
manage.py test runs under settings_test are blind to each other.

What they do share is files on disk: the item databases, and the working tree
itself, since the suite imports the files as they are and not as they were
committed. So the rule is about writes, not about suites: while a suite runs,
do not rebuild a database and do not edit a tracked file.

Running the suite WITHOUT --settings=fashionsite.settings_test is a different
trap: it falls back to MySQL, where two runs really do collide over the same
test_fashionista database, and reports around a hundred failures that say
nothing about the code.
